WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:10.870
- So as Peter mentioned,
I'm the Program Manager

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.490
for the DHLab, and one of the things

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:16.668
that I think a lot about
is how do we help students

00:00:16.668 --> 00:00:18.200
and faculty who are interested

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.700
in digital humanities methods get started.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.710
Oftentimes computation is a
new dimension to their work.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.410
They might not have experience,

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:28.030
oftentimes, they won't have
experience in programming.

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:29.270
They might or might not know

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:31.645
what the file system is on their computer.

00:00:31.645 --> 00:00:33.870
And they all have different timeframes

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.220
in which they're working
in which they need

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.150
to complete a project.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:39.140
Depending what they're hoping to do,

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.200
that poses more or less of a problem.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.400
If they're interested in getting
started with text analysis

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.760
there are a lot of really
out-of-the-box tools,

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.440
no programming required to get started.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.130
Oftentimes as an entry into later

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:53.220
sometimes more sophisticated methods.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.850
But if they're interested
in image analysis,

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:58.730
it tends to be a little
bit steeper of a curve.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:00.550
The approaches tend to
be really technically

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:02.670
and mathematically sophisticated.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:05.220
They're oftentimes working with thousands,

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.620
or the algorithms are primed
for working with thousands

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:10.450
or tens of thousands of
images as we've seen earlier,

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:12.170
and students and faculty
who are coming to me

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:14.240
usually aren't working quite at that scale

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.000
but they are interested
in these approaches.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.120
So how can they actually get started?

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.540
And as Peter mentioned, one of the reasons

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:22.770
this has been on our mind lately,

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.460
especially in the image computation space,

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.670
is we did have a student
come to us last semester.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:28.880
She's a Masters student in sculpture,

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.460
and she was interested in
working with this collection

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.370
of photographs of paintings that she has.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:36.063
It was around 65 photographs of an artist

00:01:36.063 --> 00:01:39.301
that she was close with
and she wanted to know

00:01:39.301 --> 00:01:42.420
whether she could take those
photographs of these paintings

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.960
and create new images
that would be in the style

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:46.580
of those photographs
so that they would look

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:50.450
like the artist had created
them even though he hadn't.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.930
For her this was both an artistic project

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:55.270
and it was something she
was thinking about as part

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:57.570
of her program as well
as research project,

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.097
but it was also very personal to her.

00:01:59.097 --> 00:02:00.860
This artist that she was working

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:04.220
with was a very close
friend and is deceased,

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.190
and so it's sort of a fixed collection.

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:08.300
She was really very motivated

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:11.200
and invested in working on this project.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.327
And she had seen, some
of you might have seen,

00:02:13.327 --> 00:02:16.170
that Christie's recently sold an artwork

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.310
for almost half a million dollars

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:19.920
that was produced with a Ganz so some

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.420
of the things Peter
was showing us earlier.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:23.980
We really as a lab
should be producing Ganz

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.130
I think for Christie's.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:27.620
But she was really
excited by this approach

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:29.170
and saw that it seemed like something

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:31.160
that is kind of what she was hoping to do,

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:32.994
but the problem is is Ganz do require you

00:02:32.994 --> 00:02:34.922
to have thousands and tens
of thousands of images

00:02:34.922 --> 00:02:37.810
and she had around 65,
so that wasn't going

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:39.740
to quite be an approach she could use.

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:41.590
And she herself had no
programming experience

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:44.010
so that was also going
to be a bit of a barrier.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:45.870
So we wanted to find something that could

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:47.490
at least get her started in thinking

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.330
along these computational lines

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:51.730
that could be a first
pass for this project.

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:53.647
And so neural style
transfer was the approach

00:02:53.647 --> 00:02:55.958
that we decided we would take.

00:02:55.958 --> 00:02:58.660
This is where you are
combining the content

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:00.930
of one image with the
style of another image

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.120
by way of convolutional neural networks.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.700
And we were going to use the code base

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.590
that's produced by Justin Johnson.

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:09.018
There's his Github link, and
I'll post the link again later

00:03:09.018 --> 00:03:11.110
if you're interested in it.

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:12.947
And there were several
advantages, we thought,

00:03:12.947 --> 00:03:14.960
in using this approach.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.030
It works on a small scale so it can work

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.040
with just two images though it can work

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.580
with more than two as we'll
see in just a few minutes.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:24.350
After the initial setup, it
only requires a few lines

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:26.439
of code to run, and so this meant

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:29.320
that after a session on the unit shelves

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.010
this student would be able
to run this code on her own,

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:34.200
she'd be able to change
some of the parameters

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.134
to tweak the output so she
wouldn't be dependent on us

00:03:36.134 --> 00:03:39.320
to run the algorithm and make changes,

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.570
and our best guess is she
would actually be able

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:42.720
to get her hands on the code

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.732
and make some slight
adjustments here or there

00:03:44.732 --> 00:03:48.210
based on what she was
seeing as the output.

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:49.720
And this also the ability to kind

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:52.300
of tweak the parameters incrementally

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:53.770
also meant that she could get a little bit

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:55.100
of experience working with the code

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.200
and trying to figure out
what it's actually doing

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.630
so that she could see a
little bit behind the scenes.

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:02.610
One challenge that we had to be mindful

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:04.490
of though was the filter effect.

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:07.500
So as the name neural
style transfer suggests,

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.070
what the algorithm is trying
to do is grab the content

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:13.210
from the content image, take
the style from the style image,

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:15.580
and generate a new output
image that has the content

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:17.690
of one with the style of the other.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:21.150
But that's not exactly what
our student wanted to do.

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:23.978
She didn't just want the
style of this painting,

00:04:23.978 --> 00:04:27.410
her artist's paintings,
she also wanted content

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:29.760
that felt new and generated as well.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.430
And so even though it's a
neural style transfer algorithm,

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:35.160
we were trying to think
is this still an approach

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.320
that we could use to get something

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:37.610
that feels really generative

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.280
even on the content side as well.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:44.110
And so what we did is I'm gonna
show you a few experiments

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:46.440
that I ran while we were just
trying to get our footing

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.100
with what this algorithm was capable of,

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:51.230
and then we taught her
how to do this as well

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:53.680
so that she could experiment
with her own data set.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:55.500
But instead of showing her data set,

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.070
I'm going to show some
Kandinsky paintings instead.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:01.022
So for the first experiment that we ran,

00:05:01.022 --> 00:05:02.590
we just did the defaults.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:04.020
So we took one Kandinsky painting,

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.070
we took another Kandinsky
painting, and we produced a third.

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:10.190
And as you can see, we
have, yes, we have the style

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:13.940
of that second one, we have
the colors of that second one,

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:16.050
but we still have all
the main core content

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:17.570
from that first original image.

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:19.170
And that's because, again,
we haven't changed any

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:21.533
of the default parameters at this point.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:24.840
But what we could start
doing is you could have more

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:26.880
than one style if you wanted.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:28.370
And so in this case we were taking now

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:32.900
four Kandinsky paintings as our
style input, and by default,

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:34.950
all of those style images
are equally weighted

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:38.860
but you can adjust that which
is something we'll get to.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:41.170
And so, what you're seeing
is yes we're starting

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:43.220
to see a little bit more
variation now in terms

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:44.950
of the final output colors starting

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:45.870
to look a little different

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:49.030
but that content is still
really the same content.

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:50.456
And so the next step is the one

00:05:50.456 --> 00:05:52.630
that I think starts being promising

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:54.560
for what the student was hoping to do

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:56.700
which is that you can
also adjust the weights.

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:59.040
So you can have the trade-off
between the relative weights

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.160
of how much retain of that content image

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.860
and how much you are taking
from the style images.

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:06.870
Changing the weights will
result in higher loss

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:09.081
for each of those epics, we
were just hearing about epics.

00:06:09.081 --> 00:06:10.470
But as a result of that,

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:13.350
you start to see greater
change with each iteration.

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:14.290
So you start to get something

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:16.900
that feels a little more transformative.

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:19.130
And so after that point,
after you're mixing

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:20.530
up the styles and the weights,

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:22.270
really it's just a matter
of keep tweaking it.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:25.300
Try different weight settings
and see which one starts

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:27.910
to feel more in line with
what you're hoping to see.

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:29.400
I rather like, actually, the third one.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:31.060
I think that looks like
a nice Kandinsky painting

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:33.660
even though it's not exactly.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:35.400
But what else you could
do is you could also take

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.930
from an earlier output in that
epic if you were interested.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:41.410
So this is going to cycle
through each 100 epics

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:44.340
it gives you an output
image, and so, again,

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.320
depending what the goal
is for the project,

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:49.633
you could take from one
of those earlier stages

00:06:49.633 --> 00:06:52.423
and have that be an image
that you want to work with.

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:56.420
Or the last thing that
I'll show is you could

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:59.960
take the output and use that
as your new content image.

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:02.870
So now we have an output
which is already kind

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:05.460
of a mixed up one has a lot
of things going on with it,

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:08.246
and then we're adding
three more style images

00:07:08.246 --> 00:07:10.290
to that and then result feels, again,

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:11.606
that much more transformative now

00:07:11.606 --> 00:07:14.806
because we suddenly don't
have a content image

00:07:14.806 --> 00:07:19.090
that already had a firm
content attached to it.

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:21.150
And so, I'm gonna take that last one now

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:23.120
and put it actually in
the context of a bunch

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:25.560
of Kandinsky paintings and you can see

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:27.870
that it really does start to
have the feel of Kandinsky

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:30.810
without having the exact content copied

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:32.560
from any single one of those.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:35.070
And so, this was something
that was really exciting

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:36.478
to the student that we were working with.

00:07:36.478 --> 00:07:39.430
We were able to get her
set up on the computer

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:42.454
in the cube actually is where
she did a lot of this work.

00:07:42.454 --> 00:07:44.720
So she had a key to the
cube, she was able to come in

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.520
any time Sterling was
open, run these algorithms

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.580
on her own with her own data set.

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:52.150
She could try switching out paintings

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:54.710
'cause some images work better
as content images than others

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:57.210
some work better as
style images than others.

00:07:57.210 --> 00:07:59.640
And she also is starting to think

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:00.990
about how she might expand
her corpus even more

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:04.438
to include things like
some of the photographs

00:08:04.438 --> 00:08:07.250
that the artist took that
inspired the paintings he

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:09.047
then went on to do, and so mixing some of

00:08:09.047 --> 00:08:10.846
those actual source photographs

00:08:10.846 --> 00:08:12.950
with the actual photographs themselves

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:14.360
to produce something new.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.310
But other ways that you
could tweak the parameters

00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:18.670
that I haven't shown
are you could do things

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:21.330
like rotating the style image
would have an affect on this.

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:24.930
You could choose how much
of the color you're taking

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:26.360
from the content image or not.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:29.010
You could resize the style images.

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:31.212
So the code is actually really flexible.

00:08:31.212 --> 00:08:33.067
It has a lot of different parameters

00:08:33.067 --> 00:08:34.700
that you might play around with

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:36.540
and so for anyone who is interested

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:37.790
in getting something up and running

00:08:37.790 --> 00:08:40.610
or for more of the technical backend of it

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:42.410
and is really interested in
the fact that we ran this

00:08:42.410 --> 00:08:45.081
with Cuda and Torch and
on a GPU instead of a CPU,

00:08:45.081 --> 00:08:47.500
I recommend checking
out the code on Github.

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:48.550
It's all open.

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:51.130
And then this is the paper
that inspired the code

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:53.590
so for anyone who is interested
in exploring with that.

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:54.467
Thank you.

00:08:54.467 --> 00:08:57.467
(audience applause)

